.lua
book.chapter(21, "Core and the numeric libraries")
.endlua
.sh 2 "The prelude is a convenient vocabulary"
.pp
The prelude loads portable library modules and opens Core. Common helpers such
as map, filter, fold, sum, and length become convenient unqualified names.
Qualified names remain preferable when documenting a library interface because
they identify the owner of an operation. In particular, concat in the prelude
joins strings, while List.concat flattens a list of lists. Similar names do
not imply identical types or behavior.
.pp
Core contains small functional adapters that help align interfaces without
changing the underlying computation. Identity returns its argument, const
retains the first argument, and flip reverses the first two arguments of a
curried function. Curry and uncurry adapt pair-taking and curried interfaces.
These helpers are useful when they clarify a library connection; a dense
expression of adapters can be less readable than a short named lambda.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "identity" (Core.id "x") "x"
Test.equal "constant" (Core.const 3 "ignored") 3
Test.equal "flipped subtraction" (Core.flip (fn x y -> x - y) 2 9) 7
Test.equal "prelude traversal" ([1, 2, 3] |> map (* 2) |> sum) 12
Test.equal "string concatenation" (concat ["Meta", "ML"]) "MetaML"]=],
    "Core adapters and prelude conveniences")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Choose an arithmetic contract"
.pp
Int exposes named arithmetic, comparison, bounds, conversion, bit operations,
and number-theory helpers. Its t alias is the built-in integer type. Ordinary
operations wrap in the signed 64-bit representation. Checked addition,
subtraction, and multiplication instead return None when the mathematical
answer cannot be represented. For allocation sizes and input counts, checked
arithmetic usually expresses the desired contract more accurately than wraparound.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "checked product" (Int.checked_mul 6 7) (Some 42)
Test.equal "asymmetric bound" (Int.checked_mul Int.min_value (~1)) None
Test.equal "boundary subtraction" (Int.checked_sub Int.min_value 0) (Some Int.min_value)
Test.equal "greatest common divisor" (Int.gcd 84 30) 6
Test.equal "parse hexadecimal" (Int.from_string "0xff") (Some 255)
Test.equal "reject partial input" (Int.from_string "12x") None]=],
    "Integer operations with explicit boundary behavior")
.endlua
.pp
The minimum integer has no positive counterpart in the signed range. Its
absolute value therefore remains the minimum integer under wrapping behavior.
Related extreme cases affect gcd and lcm: these functions do not introduce
arbitrary-precision arithmetic. Pow requires a nonnegative exponent. Shift
counts are masked to six bits, so a shift distance is interpreted modulo 64.
Document these details when implementing binary formats or numeric protocols.
.pp
Int.from_string accepts supported signs, bases, and separators but rejects
whitespace, incomplete parses, malformed separators, and out-of-range values.
Its Option result is appropriate for external input. A source literal is
handled by the lexer instead, and malformed syntax becomes a diagnostic.
Do not confuse a conversion function's recoverable result with the error
behavior of an invalid token embedded directly in a program.
.sh 2 "Floating operations retain IEEE distinctions"
.pp
Float provides elementary functions, rounding, classification, constants,
and conversion. Floor, ceil, and round return Float values. Truncate and
to_int return integers, saturating at the integer limits and mapping NaN to
zero. This defined boundary differs from assuming an arbitrary native cast
will behave usefully for every floating value. Explicit conversion is
particularly important in host callbacks and interchange formats.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "exact square root" (Float.sqrt 9.0) 3.0
Test.equal "rounding operations"
  (Float.floor 2.9, Float.ceil 2.1, Float.round 2.5) (2.0, 3.0, 3.0)
Test.check "infinity classification" (Float.is_infinite Float.infinity)
Test.check "NaN classification" (Float.is_nan Float.nan)
Test.equal "empty conversion" (Float.from_string "") None]=],
    "Rounding, classification, and fallible conversion")
.endlua
.pp
Float.from_string returns Option, whereas of_string reports a runtime failure
on invalid input. Numeric interpretation uses the host conversion machinery;
it is not a strict JSON-number validator. Float.to_string seeks a short
round-tripping representation and preserves negative zero. Special values
render as nan, inf, or -inf. Such strings are useful MetaML inspection text
but are not automatically valid in every external numeric format.
.sh 2 "Numerical tests should express the intended accuracy"
.pp
Exact equality is appropriate for exactly representable arithmetic or a
specified exact round trip. Approximate algorithms need a tolerance related
to their domain. An absolute tolerance handles values near zero, while a
relative tolerance scales with magnitude. Combining both is often useful.
The chosen bound should follow the algorithm's accuracy requirements rather
than an arbitrary number copied from an unrelated test.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[close actual expected :=
  Float.abs (actual - expected) <=
    0.000000001 * Float.max 1.0 (Float.abs expected)
Test.check "approximate decimal arithmetic" (close (0.1 + 0.2) 0.3)
Test.check "reject meaningful difference" (not (close 1.0 1.1))]=],
    "A combined absolute and relative comparison for finite values")
.endlua
.pp
The comparison deliberately assumes finite inputs. NaNs and infinities need
separate classification if they belong to the application's domain. A
three-way comparator is not a substitute for an accuracy policy, and sorting
NaN-containing values does not create a meaningful mathematical order.
Elementary functions can also differ at the last bit across platform math
libraries, so portability claims should distinguish same-host dialect parity
from universal bit-identical numerical results.
.sh 2 "Rendering and hashing are not serialization"
.pp
Core.show renders values for inspection, including quotes around strings and
a marker for functions. Core.hash hashes a rendered representation rather
than defining a durable, schema-independent object identity. Core.equal and
compare are structural operations with runtime restrictions on functions and
opaque resources. For long-lived caches or public data formats, choose a
canonical serialization and version its schema before hashing the bytes.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metalib/core/mod.ml, metalib/int/mod.ml, metalib/float/mod.ml, and their
runtime primitives. Write a checked rectangular allocation-size calculator
that rejects negative dimensions before multiplying. Then write a floating
mean that returns None on an empty list. Explain why checking emptiness is a
domain decision, checked multiplication is a representation decision, and a
tolerance is an accuracy decision. Keeping those questions separate makes
numeric interfaces much easier to review.
